# Horizontal scrolling

- For current and future horizontally scrolling UI, hide the scrollbar on mobile (below the shared `md` breakpoint) while preserving native touch, trackpad, mouse-wheel, and keyboard scrolling. Never use `overflow: hidden` just to hide a scrollbar.
- Reuse `max-md:[scrollbar-width:none] max-md:[&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden` on the horizontal scroll container.
- Where a desktop scrollbar is visible, leave enough bottom padding that the track never touches chips, thumbnails, text, or controls.
- Leave enough padding inside clipped scroll containers for complete keyboard focus outlines. Keep every item reachable by keyboard, and reveal focused items without moving focus unexpectedly.
- Preserve discoverability of offscreen content through a partially visible next item, visible navigation controls, or another appropriate cue. Do not rely on a hidden scrollbar as the only indication that more content exists.
- When an active item follows page scrolling, keep it visible horizontally without scrolling the page vertically or stealing focus.
- Keep touch targets at least 44px where practical, support reduced motion, and verify narrow-screen reflow and focus visibility.
- This rule governs presentation of intentional horizontal scrolling. Continue using wrapping chips where existing rules require them; do not introduce new horizontal overflow to avoid wrapping.
